Caustics by Andre Hall (Noctura)
https://github.com/DRAZY/noctura-screensaver/blob/main/src/scenes/Caustics.ts

The shader is the fragment shader embedded in src/scenes/Caustics.ts of the
Noctura screensaver (https://github.com/DRAZY/noctura-screensaver), read at
commit 721406870ecd7db07b23c1460fed4dec753dcdd1. That file carries no licence
header of its own; it is covered by the LICENSE of the repository it was written
for, and by the README. The statements, quoted exactly:

  LICENSE of the repository (https://github.com/DRAZY/noctura-screensaver/blob/main/LICENSE,
  lines 1 to 3 and the grant at line 5):

    "MIT License

     Copyright (c) 2026 Andre Hall

     Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy
     of this software and associated documentation files (the "Software"), to deal
     in the Software without restriction, including without limitation the rights
     to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell
     copies of the Software, ..."

  README.md of the repository, section "License", line 150:

    "MIT — see [LICENSE](LICENSE). Created by Andre Hall."

Third-party code inside the shader: the 2D simplex noise function snoise() is
the textureless simplex noise by Ian McEwan, Ashima Arts, and Stefan Gustavson
(https://github.com/ashima/webgl-noise), which the Noctura project credits in
src/engine/shaders/noise.glsl.ts as "Ashima Arts / Stefan Gustavson simplex
noise (MIT)". It is under the same MIT licence, and its copyright notice is
kept below as that licence requires. Its LICENSE reads, in part:

    "Copyright (C) 2011 by Ashima Arts (Simplex noise)
     Copyright (C) 2011-2016 by Stefan Gustavson (Classic noise and others)

     Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy
     of this software and associated documentation files (the "Software"), to deal
     in the Software without restriction, ..."

Ported to Metal Shading Language for MacOS Live Wallpaper; see the note at the
top of main.metal for what was changed. In short:

  - The three.js / TypeScript wrapper is removed. The uniforms uSpeed, uScale and
    uSize are constants at the scene's own defaults (0.4, 5.0, 1.0), and the
    colours are the default "Ocean" palette (#030e2e, #0c5ca3, #6bd2e0), written
    as the linear-light values three.js passes to the shader, which reproduces
    the look of the author's own screenshot.
  - main() became mainImage(); vUv is derived from fragCoord and iResolution;
    gl_FragColor and gl_FragCoord became fragColor and fragCoord.
  - The `precision` line is dropped and `x12.xy -= i1` is written as an
    assignment.
  - worley() keeps the author's 3x3 cell search and additionally searches the
    ring of cells beyond it where the second-nearest distance is above 1.0. That
    removes rare hard seams the 3x3 search alone leaves (0.04% of the pixels of a
    2056x1329 frame); every other pixel is identical to the author's result.
  - No images are used. Nothing else was changed: the maths, constants, step
    counts and speed are the author's.

